charges are pending this morning for a  Southwest Airlines pilot after TSA  agents say they found a gun in his  carry-on bag at st. Louis Airport TSA  screeners nationwide are finding more  firearms than ever and on Capitol Hill  the agency faces criticism for its poor  performance in undercover tests Kris Van  Cleave is at Reagan National Airport  just outside of Washington Chris good  morning good morning TSA officers are  finding guns at checkpoints across the  country at a rate of nearly 12 a day and  the most common explanation when it  happens passengers will say they simply  forgot the gun was in that bag TSA  screeners at st. Louis's Lambert  International Airport spotted this  loaded 9-millimeter Smith &amp; Wesson in a  carry-on of a Southwest Airlines first  officer who had planned to board a  flight to Las Vegas the 51 year old  pilot was arrested and the airport says  charges are pending  the TSA s Instagram is full of pictures  of guns found at checkpoints so far this  year officers have stopped more than  3,700 from getting through that's up ten  percent from all of last year and the  numbers have been rising for a decade  this latest incident comes as the TSA is  facing heavy criticism for missing at  least 70 percent of prohibited items  brought through checkpoints during  undercover tests earlier this year  Florida Senator Bill Nelson you've been  briefed on this report how concerning is  this I mean this is hair on fire the  testing is done every two years in 2015  the TSA failed 95% of the time what do  you think that says about the level of  security at those checkpoints at  airports across the country I'm just  amazed  that a terrorist has not come through a  checkpoint and done some very bad things  on an airplane Department of Homeland  Security Inspector General John Roth  appeared before the House Oversight  Committee yesterday we found  deficiencies in the equipment we found  deficiencies in the personnel and we  found deficiencies in the 